# Amethyst — Obsidian Compatibility Rules

## 1. Fundamental Principle

Compatibility with existing Obsidian Vaults is a core product requirement.

Amethyst must work directly with existing Vaults without requiring migration, conversion or restructuring.

A Vault opened in Amethyst must remain usable in Obsidian.

Never assume that a Vault contains only standard Markdown.

Preserve unsupported and unrecognized content rather than removing or modifying it.

## 2. Markdown Compatibility

Support standard Markdown syntax and preserve its original meaning.

Pay particular attention to:

- Headings.
- Paragraphs and line breaks.
- Ordered and unordered lists.
- Nested lists.
- Checkboxes.
- Tables.
- Blockquotes.
- Code blocks and inline code.
- Links and images.
- Emphasis and formatting.
- Horizontal rules.

Do not unnecessarily rewrite the entire Markdown document when modifying a small part of it.

Preserve formatting and syntax that the application does not need to modify.

## 3. Wikilinks

Support Obsidian-style internal links:

- `[[Note]]`
- `[[Note|Display name]]`
- `[[Note#Heading]]`
- `[[Note#Heading|Display name]]`
- `[[#Heading]]`
- `[[Note^block-id]]`

Account for:

- Links to notes in nested folders.
- Links with aliases.
- Links to headings and blocks.
- Missing or unresolved links.
- Duplicate note names.
- Configurable link resolution behavior where appropriate.

Do not assume that a link target can always be resolved using its filename alone.

Preserve the original link syntax when editing documents.

## 4. Embeds

Support Obsidian-style embeds:

- `![[Note]]`
- `![[Note#Heading]]`
- `![[image.png]]`
- `![[document.pdf]]`

Account for embeds of different file types.

Preserve embed syntax even when the embedded content cannot be rendered.

Do not replace unsupported embeds with destructive placeholders.

## 5. YAML Frontmatter and Properties

Frontmatter is an important part of Obsidian Vaults.

Support reading and editing YAML frontmatter while preserving:

- Unknown properties.
- Unsupported property types.
- Nested structures.
- Lists and arrays.
- Dates and other scalar values.
- Existing metadata that Amethyst does not understand.

Do not silently discard properties when saving a note.

Avoid unnecessary changes to YAML formatting, key ordering and unrelated values.

The visual Properties editor must modify only the properties the user intends to change.

Do not assume that every YAML value should be converted into a string.

## 6. Tags

Support Obsidian tags:

- Inline tags such as `#project`.
- Nested tags such as `#project/work`.
- Tags stored in frontmatter.

Preserve tag spelling and hierarchy.

Avoid treating text that merely resembles a tag as an actual tag without considering Markdown context.

## 7. Callouts

Support Obsidian callout syntax, including:

- Standard callouts.
- Different callout types.
- Custom titles.
- Collapsible callouts.
- Nested Markdown content.

Preserve callout types and metadata even when a particular callout style is not supported.

Do not convert callouts into ordinary blockquotes when saving a document.

## 8. Attachments and File References

Respect Obsidian's attachment conventions.

- Preserve original attachment files.
- Resolve attachment references correctly.
- Support relative paths.
- Support configured attachment folders.
- Handle filenames containing spaces and Unicode characters.
- Preserve file extensions and original formats.

Never rename or move attachments without an explicit user action.

When moving a note, account for links and embeds that may reference it.

## 9. Daily Notes and Templates

Daily Notes must remain compatible with existing Obsidian conventions.

Account for:

- Date-based note naming.
- Configurable date formats.
- Configurable Daily Notes folders.
- Existing templates.
- Internal links to daily notes.

Basic template support in the MVP must not break existing template files.

Do not silently rewrite templates into an Amethyst-specific format.

## 10. Obsidian Configuration

Do not assume that Amethyst controls the entire Vault.

Obsidian configuration and plugin files may exist inside the Vault.

- Do not delete or overwrite Obsidian configuration files.
- Do not modify plugin settings without explicit user intent.
- Do not assume that Amethyst understands every configuration option.
- Keep Amethyst-specific settings separate from Obsidian settings.

The presence of an unsupported configuration option must not prevent the Vault from opening unless it makes a specific operation unsafe.

## 11. Unsupported Obsidian Features

Some Obsidian features may not be implemented in the MVP.

Examples include:

- Third-party Obsidian plugins.
- Advanced Canvas functionality.
- Bases.
- Advanced plugin-specific metadata.
- Custom syntax introduced by individual plugins.

Unsupported features must be handled conservatively.

Preserve their underlying files and content.

Do not claim compatibility with a plugin merely because its files can be opened.

Amethyst will eventually have its own plugin API. Do not attempt to reproduce the entire Obsidian plugin API as an implicit requirement.

## 12. Source Preservation

The original Vault content must remain as intact as possible.

When editing a note:

1. Read and understand the relevant document structure.
2. Identify the exact content that needs modification.
3. Preserve unrelated Markdown and metadata.
4. Preserve unsupported syntax.
5. Verify that the resulting document remains valid and usable.

Do not normalize the entire Vault or rewrite all notes during indexing.

Indexing must be read-only with respect to original user files.

## 13. Compatibility Testing

Compatibility must be verified through dedicated tests.

Maintain test fixtures containing representative Obsidian content:

- Complex Markdown documents.
- Wikilinks and aliases.
- Heading and block references.
- Embeds.
- YAML frontmatter.
- Unknown Properties.
- Nested tags.
- Callouts.
- Attachments.
- Daily Notes.
- Templates.
- Unicode and unusual filenames.

Test both reading and writing.

Verify that opening and saving a document does not unexpectedly change unrelated content.

Where practical, test that the same Vault remains usable in Obsidian after modifications made by Amethyst.

## 14. General Rule

Obsidian compatibility is not achieved merely by supporting Markdown.

Preserve the existing Vault's structure, semantics and unsupported content.

When uncertain about the meaning of an Obsidian-specific construct, preserve it rather than guessing or converting it.